DATA NOTICE: grep

Data files (dvc imports; not stored by HeCBench, fetch with `dvc pull`):
  testcases/20000_leagues.txt, testcases/romeojuliet.txt,
  testcases/lua.lines.js.txt, testcases/passwd_perline_delboca.txt

What:
  Line-oriented text inputs for the regular-expression tests that
  runtests.sh runs:
  - 20000_leagues.txt: Jules Verne, "Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the
    Sea" (English translation).
  - romeojuliet.txt: William Shakespeare, "Romeo and Juliet".
  - lua.lines.js.txt: lines of JavaScript source code.
  - passwd_perline_delboca.txt: 17,784 plaintext passwords, one per
    line, with no usernames or email addresses.

Source:
  CUDA-grep by Brandon Kase and Manish Burman (CMU 15-418, 2012),
  https://github.com/bkase/CUDA-grep, testbench/testcases/, imported at
  upstream commit fa6630eb0c3e (byte-identical to the files HeCBench
  used to host). The upstream helper script
  testbench/testcases/get_passwds.py is not imported.

  Upstream does not say where the test files come from. The password
  file was added on 2012-05-08 ("Added some password files that pass
  all tests"), together with get_passwds.py, which extracts
  "email | password" pairs from a pipe-delimited account dump. The file
  is therefore very likely the password column of a leaked account
  database, with the email addresses removed. Which database is not
  recorded.

Copyright:
  CUDA-grep: Copyright (c) 2012 Manish Burman & Brandon Kase.
  The two novels are in the public domain.

License:
  MIT (see LICENSE in grep-cuda). No license is stated for the test
  files themselves.

Used by:
  grep-cuda, grep-hip, grep-omp, grep-sycl (runtests.sh, via `make run`)

Notes:
  HeCBench does not host these files: dvc pull fetches them from the
  upstream repository. Because passwd_perline_delboca.txt is probably
  derived from leaked credentials, consider replacing it with generated
  text.

